\subsection{Physical displacement error beyond the reduced model}
\label{sec:adaptation-full-state-h2}
Unlike the evaluated scalar reduction, the following full-state physical-error
formulation is unevaluated. It retains attitude, bias, and cross-covariance
effects and distinguishes physical error from regularized filter covariance.

\subsubsection{Sequential physical-error recursion}
For correction channel $j\in\{S,a,m\}$, let $\mat P^-_{j,k}$ be the
covariance immediately before that correction, including all earlier
updates and resets on the sample. Then
\begin{align}
 \mat K_{j,k}&=\mat P^-_{j,k}\mat C_{j,k}^{\top}
  (\mat C_{j,k}\mat P^-_{j,k}\mat C_{j,k}^{\top}+\mat R_j)^{-1},
 \label{eq:h2-Kj}\\
 \mat M_{j,k}&=\mat G_{j,k}(\Id-\mat K_{j,k}\mat C_{j,k}),
 \label{eq:h2-Mj}\\
 \mat A_k&=\mat M_{m,k}^{\epsilon_{m,k}}\mat M_{a,k}
            \mat M_{S,k}^{\epsilon_{S,k}}\mat\Phi_k,
 \label{eq:h2-Ak}
\end{align}
where $\mat G_{j,k}$ is the injection/reset Jacobian and the indicators
select actually applied corrections. This is the implemented order:
prediction, due integral correction, accelerometer, and asynchronous
magnetometer.

For physical error $\vct e=\hat{\vct x}-\vct x_{\rm true}$,
the integral correction obeys
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\vct e_k^+=\mat M_{S,k}\vct e_k^-
       -\mat G_{S,k}\mat K_{S,k}\vct S_{\rm true,k}.}
 \label{eq:h2-pseudo-error-forcing}
\end{equation}
The second term is physical wave distortion, whereas the internal Joseph
recursion includes the fictitious term $\mat K_S\mat R_S\mat K_S^{\top}$.
Thus $\mat P_{pp}$ is \emph{not automatically physical displacement MSE}.

The physical wave also need not satisfy the OU prior. For nonzero harmonic
frequency $\omega$, define
\begin{equation}
 \mat T_w(\omega)=
 \begin{bmatrix}(j\omega)^{-1}\Id\\(j\omega)^{-2}\Id\\
                 (j\omega)^{-3}\Id\\\Id\end{bmatrix},
 \qquad \vct\xi_w=\mat T_w\vct a_0.
 \label{eq:h2-wave-kinematics}
\end{equation}
With $z=e^{j\omega h}$, the prediction-mismatch forcing is
\begin{equation}
 \vct d_w=\mat E_L[\mat\Phi_L(\tau)-z\Id_{12}]
                  \mat T_w(\omega)\vct a_0,
 \label{eq:h2-ou-mismatch-forcing}
\end{equation}
where $\mat E_L$ inserts the $[v,p,S,a_w]$ block into the full state.
Both this term and Eq.~\eqref{eq:h2-pseudo-error-forcing} must enter a
physical-error calculation.

\subsubsection{Frozen periodic calculation and its limits}
For frozen coefficients and a commensurate update pattern repeating every
$N$ samples, collect the wave forcings, propagated through the appropriate
subsequent corrections, into $\mat B_{w,i}(\omega)$. The periodic harmonic
solution satisfies
\begin{equation}
 z\mat E_{i+1}=\mat A_i\mat E_i+\mat B_{w,i},\qquad
 \mat E_N=\mat E_0,\qquad \mat T_{pa,i}=\mat C_p\mat E_i,
 \label{eq:h2-harmonic-balance}
\end{equation}
where $\mat C_p$ selects displacement error. If the periodic solution
exists and the spectral integral is finite, a two-sided physical
acceleration spectral matrix $\mat S_a^{(2)}$ gives
\begin{equation}
 J_{p,\rm wave}=\frac{1}{2\pi N}\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}
 \int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\!\operatorname{tr}
   [\mat T_{pa,i}\mat S_a^{(2)}\mat T_{pa,i}^{H}]\,d\omega.
 \label{eq:h2-wave-cost}
\end{equation}
The physical sensor/bias contribution uses
\begin{align}
 \mat X_{i+1}&=\mat A_i\mat X_i\mat A_i^{\top}+\mat W_i,
   \qquad\mat X_N=\mat X_0,
 \label{eq:h2-periodic-lyapunov}\\
 J_{p,\rm noise}&=\frac1N\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}
                 \operatorname{tr}(\mat C_p\mat X_i\mat C_p^{\top}),
 \label{eq:h2-noise-cost}\\
 J_p&=J_{p,\rm noise}+J_{p,\rm wave}.
 \label{eq:h2-total-cost}
\end{align}
Here $\mat W_i$ represents actual sensor and bias disturbances, including
their propagation through corrections. It contains neither fictitious
pseudo-measurement noise nor an additional OU disturbance when the physical
wave is already represented by $\mat S_a^{(2)}$. Colored residuals require
state augmentation or spectral treatment.

\label{sec:adaptation-full-state-h2-scaling}
For fixed spectral shape, frozen gains/Jacobians at each regularizer setting,
and $\rho=\log r_S$, $s=\log\sigma_a$, amplitude enters as
\begin{equation}
 J_p(\rho,s)=J_n(\rho)+e^{2s}J_w(\rho).
 \label{eq:h2-bias-variance-form}
\end{equation}
At an interior nondegenerate optimum, the general local sensitivity is
\begin{equation}
 \frac{d\log r_S^\star}{d\log\sigma_a}
   =-\frac{J_{\rho s}}{J_{\rho\rho}}.
 \label{eq:h2-general-local-amplitude-exponent}
\end{equation}
In the actual estimator, amplitude also changes the prior covariance and
trajectory Jacobians. Evaluating those dependencies requires recomputing
the gain sequence; nonperiodic scheduling requires a trajectory-based
finite-horizon calculation. No full-state optimum, coefficient, or amplitude
exponent is claimed from this unevaluated formulation.
